Lemmings (RISC OS/Acorn Archimedes, 1992, Krisalis) - reverse engineering and
static recompilation, following the same approach as ../galactic dan.

Disc images
-----------
"Lemmings (1991)(Krisalis).adf"      819200 bytes, ADFS-E format (800K "new
                                      map", old-style small directories).
                                      Despite the .adf extension this is an
                                      Acorn ADFS floppy image, not Amiga.
"Lemmings (1991)(Krisalis)[a].adf"   814080 bytes, alternate dump, not yet
                                      investigated.

Unlike Galactic Dan's disc, this one is NOT copy protected: no encrypted
loader, no protection module/track. !Run just RMLoads two modules and runs
a boot file in the clear.

tools/                  ADFSlib.py (github.com/dcf21/adfslib, vendored) reads
                         the ADFS-E layout correctly; tools/adfsd_old.py
                         (galactic dan's old-map extractor) does NOT work on
                         this disc - wrong map format, kept only for
                         reference. extract.py rebuilds extracted/ from the
                         .adf. arm2.py is galactic dan's ARM2 emulator (used
                         both for the notes above and, now, for real work -
                         see unpack_lemboot.py below).

extracted/              !LEMMINGS/ application tree, as stored on disc:
                           !Run, !RunCold      Obey files (see below)
                           !Sprites, Boot      icon sprites / boot options
                           LemBoot             4.5K loader, RISC OS absolute
                                               load &8000. First &2000 bytes
                                               of itself get copied to
                                               &40000 (loop at &8010-&802c)
                                               and it continues running from
                                               there - which is what makes it
                                               safe to then OS_File-load
                                               LemProg *at* &8000, clobbering
                                               the vacated low copy of
                                               itself. Also loads/unpacks
                                               Krisalis, PSYGNO and DMALOGO
                                               the same way (not wired up
                                               yet, see below).
                           MemMod              relocatable module, RMLoaded
                                               by !Run before anything else
                                               ("RMensure TequeMemory 0
                                               RmLoad ... MemMod").
                           musmod2             sound driver module (RMLoaded
                                               as MusicModule2).
                           LRestMouse          tiny (56 byte) helper, run
                                               after the game exits.
                           Lemmings/           DmaLogo (intro?), Fred (4
                                               bytes - a flag?), Krisalis
                                               (splash?), LemProg (68772
                                               bytes on disc, compressed -
                                               see extracted_decompressed/),
                                               OddTable, Psygno (sound
                                               engine?), SampdatP, LemGrafP,
                                               plus:
                             LemGrafix/        ArcGrn1-5P, ArcObj1-5P,
                                               Arcpan1p, SPECIAL0-3 - sprite/
                                               graphics data. The "P" suffix
                                               plus LemBoot's unpacker
                                               strongly suggests these are
                                               compressed too (not confirmed
                                               yet - LemBoot only references
                                               Krisalis/PSYGNO/DMALOGO/
                                               LemProg by name, so either
                                               these are unpacked by a
                                               different piece of code, by
                                               LemProg itself at runtime, or
                                               under a name we haven't
                                               matched to a file yet).
                             LemLevels/        LEMMUS0/1, LEVEL000-024,
                                               LEVELDAT, MUSINFO - level
                                               data, one file per level.

extracted_decompressed/ Decompressed game files, built by tools/unpack_*.py.
                           LemProg.bin         147264 bytes, load &8000 -
                                               the REAL main engine, in
                                               plain ARM2 code (confirmed by
                                               disassembling start/middle/
                                               end - all coherent, no
                                               garbage). 2.14x the on-disc
                                               size. This is what
                                               ghidra/run_ghidra_lemprog.sh
                                               analyses, and it's a normal
                                               ARM2 binary - despite the
                                               name "LemProg" sounding like
                                               it could hide an embedded
                                               68000 emulator (this being a
                                               port from the Amiga/ST 68k
                                               original), it doesn't: this
                                               is native ARM code, written
                                               specifically for this port.
                           Krisalis.bin        32000 bytes, load &8000 - a
                           PSYGNO.bin          320x200 4bpp (16-colour, two
                           DMALOGO.bin         pixels/byte, high nibble
                                               first) splash bitmap - this
                                               is an Amiga-derived port, so
                                               16 colours, not 256; an
                                               initial 8bpp/320x100 guess
                                               rendered as a duplicated,
                                               squashed image (32000 bytes
                                               also happens to divide as
                                               320x100 8bpp) and was wrong.
                                               Rendered and visually
                                               confirmed at the correct
                                               dimensions (see docs/
                                               *_preview.png): DMALOGO is the
                                               DMA Design logo, PSYGNO is the
                                               Psygnosis logo, Krisalis.bin
                                               is the Krisalis logo. No
                                               palette recovered yet - previews
                                               are rendered as grayscale
                                               (nibble value x17).

ghidra/                  Carried over from galactic dan and confirmed
                          working on real code:
                            spider.py         recursive-descent + speculative
                                               code finder. One fix on top of
                                               galactic dan's version - see
                                               "Fixing spider's false negative"
                                               below.
                            export_ghidra.py  spider -> json (unmodified)
                            gen_asm.py        json -> relocatable GNU as
                                               (unmodified, not yet run here)
                            run_ghidra_lemboot.sh / run_ghidra_lemprog.sh
                                              import LemBoot / LemProg.bin
                                              into Ghidra at &8000, apply the
                                              spider's classification,
                                              decompile.
                                              `GHIDRA=/path ./run_ghidra_lemprog.sh`
                                              (defaults to the same
                                              ghidra_12.1.3_PUBLIC install
                                              galactic dan uses). Output
                                              goes to out/lemboot/ and
                                              out/lemprog/ (galdan.c/
                                              functions.tsv - filenames are
                                              hardcoded by
                                              RiscOSPostAnalysis.java).
                          LemBoot: 28 functions decompiled, spider/ghidra
                          agree on 2020/2072 code bytes.
                          LemProg.bin: 199 functions decompiled (up from an
                          initial 149 before the spider fix below), overall
                          code coverage 12.0% -> 19.6% of the image, ghidra/
                          spider agree on 25492/28804 code bytes.

asm/, recomp/            Empty so far - this is where galactic dan's
                          reassembler and SDL3 static recompiler live; not
                          started here.

How LemProg gets decompressed
------------------------------
tools/unpack_lemboot.py doesn't reimplement LemBoot's LZ-style codec by
hand - it *emulates LemBoot itself* (via arm2.py) far enough to load and
unpack the real file, which sidesteps every chance of a hand-transcription
bug in a bit-level compression algorithm. Concretely, for LemProg:
  - LemBoot's relocated copy (see above) contains a small function at file
    offset &8aac that: sets r8 = pointer to the string "LemProg", r9 (APCS
    "sb") = &8000 (target load address), then calls a shared loader.
  - The loader issues `OS_File 255` ("load object at the given address")
    for "<Lem$Dir>.Lemmings.LemProg" - our emulated SWI handler resolves
    that RISC OS path against extracted/ (case-insensitively, stripping the
    ",load-exec" suffix extract.py adds) and copies the real file's bytes
    into emulated memory at &8000.
  - It then calls the unpacker at file offset &8468 (the same LZ-style
    routine visible from &84a4-&8620 in a disassembly - self-modifying,
    reads the compressed stream backwards from high to low addresses).
  - We run the emulator until it returns, tracking every memory write (via
    arm2.py's write_hook) to discover the true decompressed extent, then
    save that span as the output file - no assumptions about the codec's
    internals required, only about *which registers/strings set up the
    call*, which we got from reading the disassembly once.
Krisalis/PSYGNO/DMALOGO use a second, near-identical call site (file offset
&8af0, shared by all three) that reads r9 from a literal-pool slot at &8a20
instead of using a fixed constant - real LemBoot presumably fills that slot
in dynamically before each call (not traced), but all three decompress
cleanly when we pre-seed it to &8000 ourselves, same as LemProg's target.
The result for each is a 320x200 4bpp (16-colour) bitmap that renders as
the expected logo (see docs/*_preview.png) - about as strong a correctness
check on the whole codec-by-emulation approach as we could ask for.

LemGrafix/LemLevels are NOT loaded by LemBoot at all - grepping strings out
of the decompressed LemProg.bin turns up "LemGrafix.ArcGrn1P",
"LemLevels.LEVEL000" etc. around file offset &2be00-&2bf40 (right near the
end of LemProg's own data). Found the actual loader by searching the whole
binary for 4-byte words equal to each string's address (i.e. finding literal
pool references rather than relying on spider/Ghidra to have already
classified the caller as reached code - the calling function, around
&27ad0-&27ca8, is genuinely valid code that spider's speculative pass
missed, most likely because it's reached only via a jump table or function
pointer we haven't found the source of yet). Each call site: loads r8 =
string pointer via the same "bl to a fixed thunk, literal word follows"
trick LemBoot uses (&8074 = load-into-r8 thunk, &8080 = load-into-sb
thunk), patches 1-3 digit bytes into the string (level number / variant
selection - LEVEL000's digits sit at string offsets &f-&11), sets sb = a
fixed target address (LEVEL data -> &4773c/&4973c, ArcObj1P -> &6a83c,
the ArcGrn/arcpan group -> &77b2c - all comfortably above LemProg's own
&8000-&2bf40 code+data, i.e. these get their own permanent buffers rather
than being chain-loaded over running code), then tail-jumps into a loader
at &280e0 that is *the same OS_File-255-load template* LemBoot uses
(&2811c builds "<Lem$Dir>.Lemmings.<name>", svc &20008 loads it) - but,
unlike LemBoot's version, &280e0 does NOT call an unpacker afterward.
That's a real finding, not just an oversight: LEVEL000's raw on-disc bytes
don't look like the LZ-compressed streams we saw for LemProg/Krisalis/etc.
(no long zero run, fairly uniform byte distribution) and 1332 bytes is
in the right ballpark for the well-documented classic Lemmings binary
level format (terrain pieces + object placements) stored directly. So the
"P" suffix on the LemGrafix filenames most likely does NOT mean
"LZ-packed" the way DMALOGO/PSYGNO/Krisalis were - more likely a planar/
packed-pixel bitmap encoding specific to the graphics code, unrelated to
LemBoot's unpacker. Reading LemProg's sprite-drawing code (not yet
attempted) would confirm this either way.

Fixing spider's false negative on the &27a60 loader dispatch
--------------------------------------------------------------
The &27ad0-&27ca8 caller wasn't in spider's output at all initially, and
adding it as an explicit entry point (export_ghidra.py's `[extra-entry]`
args) didn't help either - the very first instruction after the entry,
&27a64, tripped spider's `valid_arm2()` check ('NV condition' = invalid)
and the whole trace was thrown away.

That word (bytes 00 00 a0 f1, i.e. "mov r0,r0" with its condition field
forced to NV = "never execute") turns out to be a real, pervasive idiom in
this codebase: "stmdb sp!, {pc}" (push just PC - a hand-rolled fake return
address, standing in for a BL whose target is out of B/BL's +-32MB range)
is always immediately followed by exactly this NV filler word, then the
real "b target". Nothing on ARM2 ever executes an NV-conditioned
instruction, so it's inert - but spider's gap analysis (correctly, for its
original job of scoring "does this look like code") flagged it as
implausible, and its recursive-descent tracer (incorrectly, for actually
disassembling this codebase) aborted the whole function on it.

We'd already seen this exact 3-word shape work fine dozens of times
elsewhere (every register-load vector thunk in LemBoot uses it) - it just
happened that in those cases spider always arrived at the idiom via an
already-established trace rather than as a fresh function entry, and
apparently never via *speculative* entry either, so the bug never showed
up as a rejected trace before.

Fixed in spider.py's LDM/STM handling: recognise "stmdb sp!, {pc}"
(register list == {pc} exactly) followed by an NV word followed by a real
B/BL, and skip the filler word instead of decoding it. Verified this isn't
over-matching ordinary data that happens to start the same way (checked
with and without also requiring the trailing B/BL - identical results
either way, so the trailing check is a safety margin, not load-bearing).

Effect on LemProg.bin alone: speculative code coverage 12.0% -> 19.6% of
the whole image, functions found 149 -> 221 (199 -> Ghidra-decompiled,
Ghidra's own jump-table warnings roughly doubled too - expected, since a
lot of the newly-found code is exactly this kind of hand-written indirect
dispatch that Ghidra can't auto-resolve, not a sign the new code is wrong).
LemBoot picked up a smaller, consistent improvement (23 -> 28 functions).
This is very likely THE reason large parts of LemProg were unreachable in
the first place, not a one-off - the same macro/idiom is presumably used
everywhere in this codebase that a far branch or call is needed.

Status / next steps
--------------------
1. Extraction: DONE (tools/extract.py, via ADFSlib).
2. LemBoot: DONE - spider+Ghidra pipeline runs clean, and we've read enough
   of the decompile to fully understand its relocate-then-chainload
   behaviour and how it invokes the unpacker for LemProg, Krisalis, PSYGNO
   and DMALOGO (see above; all four decompress and, for the three images,
   visually check out).
3. LemProg: DONE for this stage - decompressed (extracted_decompressed/
   LemProg.bin, 147264 bytes) and imported into Ghidra (205 functions,
   see above). This is the real engine, load &8000, entry point at file
   offset &192ac (i.e. runtime address &192ac). It is NOT 68000 code (it's
   a native ARM2 rewrite, not an emulated Amiga/ST port) - confirmed by
   disassembling the start, a middle section, and the very end of the
   decompressed buffer, all clean ARM2 with no garbage or 68k-style
   patterns.
4. MemMod and musmod2 are RISC OS *modules*, not absolute-loaded code - they
   have a different header format (module header with offsets to title,
   help string, SWI table, entry points) and get relocated at RMLoad time.
   spider.py/export_ghidra.py assume absolute load; will need adapting for
   the module header and for being entered via multiple SWI/vector entry
   points rather than one.
5. LemGrafix/*P and LemLevels/* loading: DONE for this stage - found the
   loader (LemProg file offset &280e0, called via &27ad0-&27ca8) by
   searching the binary for literal-pool references to the string
   addresses rather than relying on spider/Ghidra's reachability analysis,
   since that code wasn't in the (at the time) 205 already-decompiled
   functions. Chasing *why* it wasn't reachable led to the spider.py fix
   above, which pulled the whole &27a60-&2be24 region (and a lot more
   elsewhere) into the decompile properly - see ghidra/out/lemprog/galdan.c
   around those addresses for the real, now-named functions instead of the
   manual disassembly notes above. It's the same OS_File-255 template as
   LemBoot's loader, but with no unpack call - these assets most likely
   aren't LZ-compressed at all (see above).
   NEXT: run tools/unpack_lemboot.py's technique against LemProg.bin itself
   (driving arm2.py from its &280e0 entry, same OS_File SWI shim) to
   actually load a level + its graphics into a file - not needed to prove
   the format (we're not decoding it right now), but needed to get the raw
   bytes out for anything downstream. Actual level-format decoding is
   explicitly deferred per current priorities (finding code > decoding
   data).
6. Only after LemProg (+ MemMod + musmod2) are disassembled and reassemble
   byte-identically (galactic dan's asm/ milestone) does static
   recompilation to C (recomp/, galactic dan's gen_c.py) become relevant.

This is comparable in scope to galactic dan, possibly larger (multiple
cooperating modules instead of one blob, plus a real level-data format to
reverse), but with no copy protection to defeat first.
